# Executive research state
**Consolidated 18 September 2026.** Main decision: retain the existing Paper B and develop **two prospective mathematical papers**, not five loosely overlapping articles. The strongest prospective center is a chain-ring resource classification. The second is exact characteristic-two modal query complexity. Both need final priority clearance; neither should claim that CM terminology makes standard mathematics new.

## What survives the audit

The current Paper B supplies a consistent typed Boolean operator calculus. Its one-hot contraction, signed-frame fusion, formula-valued LM construction and valuation-natural pairing are foundations, not new results for subsequent papers. Its most defensible computational proposition is a precisely admitted optimization pipeline whose total costs can be measured against strong generic truth-function/cut baselines. It does not currently establish a completed confirmatory performance advantage. [S-B sections 2-8; CM-B-001--007]

The rotation lift has a real but narrower role: choosing a cyclic order on four CM coefficients gives the regular cyclic algebra A=F2[u]/u^4, and rotation equivariance selects GL_m(A). This identifies a useful restricted gate family. The centralizer, nilpotent filtration and parity/unit criterion are established algebra or direct consequences. The lift does not preserve either the original 2x2 CM product or entrywise Boolean AND. In particular, the ordinary identity CM lifts to the square-zero element u^2. [S-ADV D1-D3; S-CAP F1; ROT-001--009]

The strongest resource theorem classifies a nonzero bipartite matrix over a finite commutative chain ring by its Smith exponents. Let r be the number of nonzero Smith factors and h the multiplicity of the smallest exponent. With **all primitive projective local bases** and the rule nonzero amplitude = possible, r=1 is local, r>=2 and h=1 is logical-but-not-strong, and h>=2 is strongly contextual. This is an analytic theorem, not merely a 16-element-ring enumeration. [S-ADV D4-D5; MOD-003--005]

Under a matched square-resource contract, exact invertible-orbit dense coding has maximum **d*h messages**. Thus strong contextuality and a dense-coding advantage over d coincide in that contract. Universal exact teleportation, by contrast, requires an invertible resource, and sufficiency also requires an admissible complete invertible-matrix analyzer. The supplied constructions provide such analyzers in dimensions 2 and 8. A nonzero nilpotent scalar multiple of I_d maximizes the codebook while failing teleportation. This example is valuable but **uses nonprimitive resources**; it is excluded if initial preparations must be primitive. A primitive dimension-three example retains nonmaximal dense advantage without full-rank teleportation. [S-CAP R2-R4; RES-001--008]

The algorithmic center is not a collection of unsuccessful quantum imitations. Under the standard XOR oracle and exact all-outcomes-correct linear modal readout, Deutsch requires exactly 2 queries and Bernstein-Vazirani requires exactly n. Deutsch-Jozsa has a general one-query obstruction and only the stated bounds 2 <= Q <= 2^(n-1)+1. The lower-bound arguments tolerate arbitrary ancillas and complete recorded adaptive linear branches. An important consolidation clarification is that the **upper bounds require an informative oracle such as XOR**; arbitrary invertible G0,G1 can coincide and then reveal no information at any query count. [S-CAP Q1-Q4; ALG-001--004; correction C17]

Actual phase kickback exists over A, but the proposed two-character matrix is singular: its n-fold shared tensor has binary rank 4+2n rather than 4*2^n. This separates the resource of kickback from the resource of an invertible Fourier readout. The known Willcock-Sabry UNIQUE-SAT construction is retained as a positive control, not renamed as a discovery: it prevents an unjustified blanket conclusion that modal computation never has an oracle advantage. [S-CAP Q5 and UNIQUE-SAT; ALG-005--010]

## What the LM bridge establishes

There is a sufficient, clean interface, not a canonical derivation of modal theory from Paper B. After choosing A, enrich the Boolean formula ring to B tensor_F2 A = B[u]/u^4. Extend valuations coefficientwise. A symbolic coefficient q=sum f_j u^j has possibility formula OR_j f_j, whose Boolean valuation equals the nonzero test on its evaluated A coefficient. Basis-projector updates B0^-1 P_i B0 are division-free and valuation-natural. The scalar extension, effects, support rule and tensor contract remain additional choices. This is useful semantic engineering and connective tissue for P01; it is not presently a compelling separate article. [S-LMB; LMB-001--008]

The operational limitation is substantive. All nonzero shared-ring preparations are not closed under independent tensor composition; primitive-only preparations are not closed under conditioning. The static classifications and explicitly checked protocols remain meaningful, but they do not by themselves define a closed normalized process theory. [MOD-001--002; NEG-002]

